You’re seeing “price explosions” because the quick fix (point-wise flooring vol/variance to be >0) creates tiny, sharp k/T-spikes in the surface. Those are invisible in factor/QQ plots but blow up option prices once you (a) convert to σ(k,T)=√(w/T) and (b) interpolate/extrapolate across strikes/maturities.

The cure is: stop clipping; instead do a global projection/repair of the total-variance surface that (i) keeps you close to the raw reconstruction, (ii) enforces all static-arb constraints, and (iii) smooths the surface so there are no micro-spikes.

Below is an exact, practical recipe.

⸻

Why flooring causes explosions (quick intuition)
	•	Flooring at a few nodes turns tiny negative w into a positive step.
	•	After √(·)/T and interpolation, that step becomes a kink → local density spikes → far-OTM call/put prices jump.
	•	With small T (e.g., 1M), any irregularity in w is magnified in σ and then in prices (vega is largest around ATM).

⸻

What to do instead (robust repair of w)

Let w_{\text{raw}}(k,T)=w_0(k,T)+B_{\text{today}}(k,T)\,\Delta s be your reconstructed total variance on a fixed log-moneyness grid k\in\{k_1<\dots<k_M\} and maturities T\in\{T_1<\dots<T_N\}.

Project to the nearest admissible surface:
\min_{w}\ \sum_{i,j} \alpha_{ij}\,\big[w_{ij}-w_{\text{raw},ij}\big]^2
\;+\;\lambda_k \sum_{j}\!\sum_{i} (\Delta^2_k w_{ij})^2
\;+\;\lambda_T \sum_{i}\!\sum_{j} (\Delta^2_T w_{ij})^2
subject to, for all grid points:
	1.	Positivity: w_{ij} \ge \varepsilon_T with \varepsilon_T = \varepsilon_0\,T_j. (E.g., \varepsilon_0=10^{-8} ⇒ σ_min ≈ 10^{-4}.)
	2.	Calendar monotonicity: \Delta_T w_{i,j} := w_{i,j+1}-w_{i,j} \ge 0.
	3.	Strike-convexity (no butterfly): \Delta^2_k w_{i,j} := w_{i-1,j}-2w_{i,j}+w_{i+1,j} \ge 0.
(This enforces convexity of prices when you later interpolate linearly in price or w across k.)
	4.	Wing slope caps (Lee bounds):
\frac{w_{M,j}-w_{m,j}}{k_M-k_m} \le 2-\delta,
\quad
\frac{w_{m,j}-w_{1,j}}{k_m-k_1} \le 2-\delta
for chosen wing anchors k_1\ll0, k_M\gg0 (or enforce locally on last intervals). Take \delta\approx 0.05.
	5.	(Optional) Temporal smoothness: penalize (\Delta_T^2 w)^2 as above to kill “saw-tooth” in T.
	6.	(Optional) Leverage correction: if you know you under/over-react to \Delta \log S, add a deterministic slice
w \leftarrow w + \Gamma(k,T)\,\Delta\log S before the projection. (Pre-fit \Gamma by OLS.)

Use larger weights \alpha_{ij} near ATM and liquid tenors so the repair preserves those best.

Important: Do not clip nodes individually. Let the optimizer move the surface minimally to satisfy all constraints and smoothness.

⸻

Minimal implementation sketch (cvxpy)
	•	Decision vector: all w_{ij} stacked.
	•	Build sparse finite-difference matrices for \Delta_k,\Delta^2_k,\Delta_T,\Delta^2_T.
	•	Quadratic objective (L2 closeness + Tikhonov smoothness).
	•	Linear inequality constraints (monotone, convex, Lee slopes, positivity).
	•	Solve with OSQP.

