⸻

Additional guardrails to stop extremes
	1.	Interpolate properly: across k use piecewise-linear in price (or in w), not in σ. Across T use monotone interpolation in total variance.
	2.	Wing extrapolation: beyond your k-grid, linear in w with slopes capped at Lee’s bound. Don’t extrapolate σ directly.
	3.	Shock winsorizing (factor space): clip factor shocks at, say, 3–4 historical σ before reconstruction (or scale them to match your empirical POT tail). This prevents a single path from forcing the projector into extreme wings.
	4.	ATM anchor: before projection, re-anchor the ATM level per maturity to your intended ATM move (e.g., match \Delta w(0,T) to the model’s ATM factor). This keeps prices from drifting due to tiny ATM mis-calibration.
	5.	Numerical floor by T: the positivity floor should scale with T: w_{\min}(T)=\varepsilon_0\,T. A constant floor in σ is a constant floor in w/T and can distort short maturities.

⸻

Quick triage checklist (run once to locate the culprit)
	•	Plot \Delta_k w and \Delta^2_k w heatmaps before/after projection. Any big isolated values? That’s where prices explode.
	•	Check wing slopes (w(k_{\text{wing}})-w(k_0))/(k_{\text{wing}}-k_0). Are they >~2? Cap them.
	•	Compare ATM 1M w series (real vs sim). If ATM is fine but prices still explode, it’s almost always a wing convexity artifact.
	•	Reprice your “exploding” day with the repaired surface; vega-normalize the price change to verify the fix.

⸻

Bottom line
	•	Flooring (positivity) alone is not enough and often creates price spikes.
	•	Replace it with a global projection in total-variance space that enforces positivity + calendar + strike-convexity + wing bounds, with small smoothness penalties.
	•	Interpolate in price or total variance, extrapolate linearly in w, and scale your floor with T.

